<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cersobleptes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 359–341 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Kersebleptes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cersobleptes was a son of Cotys I and the most powerful of the three kings among whom the Odrysian Kingdom was divided in 360 BCE, ruling its eastern part. Aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> commander Charidemus, he contested the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> before ceding it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> defeated him in 342–341 BCE and annexed most of his lands, ending Odrysian independence for a time.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cersobleptes, also written Cersebleptes or Kersobleptes, inherited the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a> on the death of his father Cotys I in September 360 BCE. With it came a conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and with the rebel former royal treasurer Miltokythes, and two rivals soon appeared, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berisades">Berisades</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadocus-ii">Amadocus II</a>. An inscription from Delphi dated to 355 BCE names four sons of his: Iolaus, Poseidonius, Medistas and Teres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Despite the service of his brother-in-law, the Euboean adventurer Charidemus, Cersobleptes had to make peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and accept his rivals as rulers of their own parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> by 357 BCE. His share lay east of the rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tundzha">Tonzos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Hebrus</a>. Charidemus led the dealings with Athens over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a>, and in them Cersobleptes appears as little more than his puppet; Athens sent settlers there only in 353 BCE. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berisades">Berisades</a> died and was followed by his sons, Cetriporis among them, the two men planned to seize their lands and win back all of Cotys's realm, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a> spoke against an Athenian decree passed in Charidemus's favour.</p><p>Around 354 or 353 BCE his envoy Apollonides negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> at Maroneia, but the plan failed, and Cersobleptes instead allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which gave up its protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadocus-ii">Amadocus II</a>. He then attacked Amadocus and the Greek cities of Perinthus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. In 352 BCE Philip invaded as their ally, defeated him and took one of his sons as a hostage to Pella.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 347 and 346 BCE Cersobleptes again sought Athenian support, but his inclusion in the peace with Philip in 346 BCE was refused, and Philip expelled the Athenian garrisons from the Thracian coast. During Philip's larger campaign, which by early 341 BCE had passed through Cardia and Aenus and taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Cabyle</a>, Cersobleptes and Teres III were eliminated or subjugated in circumstances that are unknown, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> and Cabyle became Macedonian colonies. It is unclear whether his sons kept any authority. The next important Odrysian king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a>, is sometimes identified, hypothetically, as his son or brother.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cersobleptes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his murder in 360 BCE the realm was divided between three rulers, <b>Cersobleptes</b> in the east, Amadocus II in the centre and Berisades in the west, a fragmentation that Athens and Macedon both exploited.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
